# --- Software Engineering: Chapter 5 ---
SOFTWARE_ENGINEERING_CHAPTER_5_QUIZ = [
{"q":"What is a Software Process?","o":["An organized set of activities used to develop, deliver, maintain, and evolve software","Only the act of writing code","A database backup procedure","A user-interface color scheme"],"a":"An organized set of activities used to develop, deliver, maintain, and evolve software"},
{"q":"Why are Software Processes important?","o":["They organize work, clarify responsibilities, control risks, improve quality, and make progress easier to manage","They guarantee that no defect can ever occur","They remove the need for users","They replace all testing"],"a":"They organize work, clarify responsibilities, control risks, improve quality, and make progress easier to manage"},
{"q":"Which list contains the four generic Software Process activities?","o":["Specification, Design and Implementation, Validation, and Evolution","Login, Payment, Reports, and Backup","Planning, Marketing, Hiring, and Sales","Compilation, Printing, Uploading, and Shutdown"],"a":"Specification, Design and Implementation, Validation, and Evolution"},
{"q":"What is Software Specification?","o":["Defining what the system should do, its requirements, constraints, behavior, and user needs","Writing only the user interface code","Deploying the final system","Repairing a server"],"a":"Defining what the system should do, its requirements, constraints, behavior, and user needs"},
{"q":"What is Software Validation?","o":["Checking that the system satisfies requirements, works correctly, and meets real user needs","Choosing a programming language","Creating a project budget only","Searching for reusable libraries"],"a":"Checking that the system satisfies requirements, works correctly, and meets real user needs"},
{"q":"What is Software Evolution?","o":["Changing software after delivery to fix bugs, add features, improve performance, and address new security or business needs","Deleting the system after deployment","Writing requirements once and never changing them","Only installing the operating system"],"a":"Changing software after delivery to fix bugs, add features, improve performance, and address new security or business needs"},
{"q":"What is a Software Process Model?","o":["A simplified representation that organizes development activities and their relationships","A physical server model","A database table","A programming variable"],"a":"A simplified representation that organizes development activities and their relationships"},
{"q":"What is the main characteristic of the Waterfall Model?","o":["Development proceeds through largely sequential phases, with each phase depending on outputs from earlier phases","The system is built only from existing APIs","No requirements are documented","Every feature is released at the same time without planning"],"a":"Development proceeds through largely sequential phases, with each phase depending on outputs from earlier phases"},
{"q":"Which sequence represents a typical Waterfall flow?","o":["Requirements → Design → Implementation → Testing → Deployment → Maintenance","Testing → Requirements → Deployment → Design","Deployment → Coding → Requirements → Maintenance","Design → Marketing → Hiring → Testing"],"a":"Requirements → Design → Implementation → Testing → Deployment → Maintenance"},
{"q":"When is Waterfall usually more suitable?","o":["When requirements are clear and stable, changes are limited, and formal documentation or regulation is important","When requirements change every day and users need constant experiments","When the team wants to avoid planning","When the project consists only of unknown prototypes"],"a":"When requirements are clear and stable, changes are limited, and formal documentation or regulation is important"},
{"q":"What is a major disadvantage of Waterfall?","o":["Late changes can be expensive, testing and user feedback may occur relatively late, and early misunderstandings may appear late","It cannot produce documentation","It never defines requirements","It always uses external components"],"a":"Late changes can be expensive, testing and user feedback may occur relatively late, and early misunderstandings may appear late"},
{"q":"What is Incremental Development?","o":["Building and delivering the system in smaller increments, with each increment adding useful functionality","Building the entire system once with no feedback","Using only third-party services","Replacing testing with documentation"],"a":"Building and delivering the system in smaller increments, with each increment adding useful functionality"},
{"q":"What is an Increment?","o":["A working addition to the system that contributes functionality toward the final product","A defect report only","A deployment failure","A database password"],"a":"A working addition to the system that contributes functionality toward the final product"},
{"q":"Which is an advantage of Incremental Development?","o":["Early delivery, early feedback, lower risk, and the ability to adjust later increments","It prevents all architecture decisions","It eliminates integration work","It requires stable requirements at the beginning"],"a":"Early delivery, early feedback, lower risk, and the ability to adjust later increments"},
{"q":"Which is a possible disadvantage of Incremental Development?","o":["Poor planning can create architectural, management, dependency, or integration problems","Users can never see working software","Testing must always happen only at the end","Requirements can never be changed"],"a":"Poor planning can create architectural, management, dependency, or integration problems"},
{"q":"What is Software Reuse?","o":["Using existing software components such as libraries, frameworks, APIs, packages, or services in a new system","Copying every source file without evaluation","Avoiding all external dependencies","Writing every component from zero"],"a":"Using existing software components such as libraries, frameworks, APIs, packages, or services in a new system"},
{"q":"What is a risk of Reuse-Oriented Development?","o":["The component may impose requirements, compatibility constraints, external dependence, or limited control","It always makes the system impossible to test","It never saves time","It removes the need for integration"],"a":"The component may impose requirements, compatibility constraints, external dependence, or limited control"},
{"q":"Which factor is important when selecting a Process Model?","o":["Requirements stability, project size, risk, team, technology, customer involvement, time, regulation, and expected change","Only the color of the application logo","Only the number of source-code files","Only the developer's favorite editor"],"a":"Requirements stability, project size, risk, team, technology, customer involvement, time, regulation, and expected change"},
{"q":"What does Iteration mean?","o":["Repeating development activities across versions to learn from feedback and improve the system","Deleting all earlier versions","Writing requirements without testing","Using a single fixed release"],"a":"Repeating development activities across versions to learn from feedback and improve the system"},
{"q":"Why is Process Measurement useful?","o":["Metrics such as development time, defects, cost, productivity, test results, and delivery time help evaluate and improve the process","It guarantees that requirements never change","It replaces stakeholder communication","It prevents all maintenance"],"a":"Metrics such as development time, defects, cost, productivity, test results, and delivery time help evaluate and improve the process"},
]
