{"q":"What is an Iteration in Agile development?","o":["A repeated cycle of planning, development, testing, review, feedback, and improvement","A complete project with no next version","A database transaction","A single coding statement"],"a":"A repeated cycle of planning, development, testing, review, feedback, and improvement"},
{"q":"What is Incremental Delivery?","o":["Delivering useful working additions to the product over multiple releases","Delivering only a final product after all work is complete","Replacing all testing with prototypes","Developing unrelated products in parallel"],"a":"Delivering useful working additions to the product over multiple releases"},
{"q":"What is Extreme Programming (XP)?","o":["An Agile method emphasizing engineering practices such as testing, feedback, simple design, refactoring, and continuous integration","A project-management role in Scrum","A database framework","A contract-negotiation technique"],"a":"An Agile method emphasizing engineering practices such as testing, feedback, simple design, refactoring, and continuous integration"},
{"q":"What is a User Story?","o":["A concise requirement expressed from a user's perspective, often using As a user, I want a feature, so that a benefit","A source-code class","A server-monitoring chart","A legal contract"],"a":"A concise requirement expressed from a user's perspective, often using As a user, I want a feature, so that a benefit"},
{"q":"What are Acceptance Criteria?","o":["Conditions that must be satisfied for a User Story to be considered complete and acceptable","A list of programming languages","A record of team attendance","A backup schedule"],"a":"Conditions that must be satisfied for a User Story to be considered complete and acceptable"},
{"q":"What is Test-First Development?","o":["Writing a test before implementing the code, then making the test pass and refactoring safely","Testing only after the entire project is deployed","Writing code without defining expected behavior","Testing documentation instead of software"],"a":"Writing a test before implementing the code, then making the test pass and refactoring safely"},
{"q":"What is Refactoring?","o":["Improving the internal structure of code without changing its externally observable behavior","Adding a new feature without testing","Deleting all tests","Changing business requirements without discussion"],"a":"Improving the internal structure of code without changing its externally observable behavior"},
{"q":"What is Pair Programming?","o":["Two developers collaborate on the same code, commonly with one acting as Driver and the other as Navigator","Two teams working on unrelated products","A customer writing all production code","A process for merging databases"],"a":"Two developers collaborate on the same code, commonly with one acting as Driver and the other as Navigator"},
{"q":"What is Continuous Integration?","o":["Frequently integrating code changes and running automated tests to detect integration problems early","Deploying once a year without tests","Writing code without version control","Keeping every developer's code permanently separate"],"a":"Frequently integrating code changes and running automated tests to detect integration problems early"},
{"q":"What is Scrum?","o":["An Agile framework for organizing and managing work through roles, artifacts, events, and Sprints","A programming language","A testing library only","A replacement for all engineering practices"],"a":"An Agile framework for organizing and managing work through roles, artifacts, events, and Sprints"},
{"q":"What is the Product Owner responsible for?","o":["Maximizing product value and managing and ordering the Product Backlog","Writing every line of code","Removing all automated tests","Managing only the daily meeting"],"a":"Maximizing product value and managing and ordering the Product Backlog"},
{"q":"What is the difference between Product Backlog and Sprint Backlog?","o":["The Product Backlog contains all known product work; the Sprint Backlog contains the work selected for the current Sprint","They are always identical","The Product Backlog contains only bugs while Sprint Backlog contains only features","Sprint Backlog is created after the project ends"],"a":"The Product Backlog contains all known product work; the Sprint Backlog contains the work selected for the current Sprint"},
{"q":"What is a Sprint?","o":["A short, time-boxed development period during which the team creates a usable product Increment","The complete lifetime of a product","A meeting that happens once a year","A list of all future requirements"],"a":"A short, time-boxed development period during which the team creates a usable product Increment"},
